index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..744f9707baca092850196c23db975956e55eb4ba
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,110 @@
+# Grand Tour witness generator (arXiv:2508.11570)
+
+This module generates **Grand Tour** instances from Kiatchaipipat and Ruangwises, [“NP-Completeness Proofs of Puzzles using the T-Metacell Framework”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.11570). The solver receives a full orthogonal square grid and a set of mandatory (“forced”) edges, then returns one cyclic ordering that visits every grid vertex exactly once and contains every forced edge. `verify` checks length, range, uniqueness, grid adjacency, closure, and the forced-edge subset in linear time.
+
+## Why this regime is hard
+
+Section 2.1 supplies the exact Grand Tour definition. Its asymmetric 5×5 forced-edge T-metacell has a unique traversal for each exit choice, which the Section 1.1 framework turns into ASP-completeness; the construction explicitly has at least one forced edge incident to every vertex. Section 2.1 also proves NP-completeness when the forced/unforced ratio is arbitrarily small. The generator uses exactly one forced incidence per vertex: it samples a Hamiltonian cycle first, then publishes one alternating perfect matching of that cycle. The paper gives no polynomial-time, closed-form, FPT, or approximation algorithm for this regime.
+
+The avoided easy extremes matter. With no forced edges, this full even grid has an immediate serpentine tour. At the other extreme, Section 2.1 notes that forcing both cycle incidences induces the cycle itself. Exactly one forced incidence leaves the hard global task: choose a complementary grid perfect matching whose union with the forced matching is one cycle, rather than many disjoint cycles.
+
+## Worked standard instance
+
+This is `make_instance(n=8, seed=0)`, the smallest shipped preset, rendered in full:
+
+```text
+Grand Tour witness problem
+
+The graph is a 16-row by 16-column rectangular grid of 256 vertices.
+Rows are 0 through 15, columns are 0 through 15, and the vertex
+at (row r, column c) has ID r*16+c.  Thus IDs are 0 through 255.
+Two vertices have an available undirected edge exactly when their grid cells share
+a side: their coordinates differ by 1 in exactly one coordinate and agree in the
+other.  Diagonal and wraparound edges do not exist.
+
+Find one simple closed loop that visits every vertex exactly once and uses every
+forced edge below.  A forced edge a-b is undirected.  The forced edges are:
+0-16 1-2 3-4 5-6 7-23 8-24 9-10 11-12 13-14 15-31 17-18 19-20 21-22 25-26 27-28 29-30 32-48 33-49 34-50 35-51 36-52 37-38 39-40 41-57 42-58 43-59 44-60 45-46 47-63 53-54 55-56 61-62 64-80 65-66 67-83 68-84 69-85 70-86 71-87 72-88 73-74 75-76 77-93 78-94 79-95 81-82 89-90 91-92 96-112 97-113 98-114 99-100 101-117 102-118 103-119 104-120 105-106 107-108 109-110 111-127 115-116 121-122 123-124 125-126 128-144 129-145 130-146 131-132 133-149 134-150 135-151 136-152 137-153 138-154 139-140 141-142 143-159 147-148 155-156 157-158 160-176 161-162 163-164 165-166 167-168 169-170 171-172 173-174 175-191 177-178 179-180 181-182 183-184 185-186 187-188 189-190 192-208 193-209 194-210 195-211 196-212 197-198 199-200 201-202 203-219 204-220 205-221 206-222 207-223 213-214 215-216 217-218 224-240 225-226 227-228 229-245 230-246 231-232 233-249 234-250 235-236 237-238 239-255 241-242 243-244 247-248 251-252 253-254
+
+Represent the loop by exactly 256 comma-separated vertex IDs v0,...,v255
+in cyclic order.  Every ID must occur exactly once.  Consecutive IDs must share an
+available edge, including v255 back to v0.  The starting vertex and direction
+are arbitrary; do not repeat v0 at the end.
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, as comma-separated integers.
+Example: <answer>0, 1, 5, 4</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+One answer is:
+
+```text
+0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 23, 39, 40, 41, 57, 56, 55, 71, 87, 103, 119, 118, 102, 86, 70, 54, 53, 69, 85, 84, 68, 52, 36, 37, 38, 22, 21, 20, 19, 35, 51, 50, 34, 18, 17, 33, 49, 65, 66, 67, 83, 99, 100, 101, 117, 116, 115, 131, 132, 133, 149, 148, 147, 163, 164, 165, 166, 150, 134, 135, 151, 167, 168, 152, 136, 137, 153, 169, 170, 171, 172, 156, 155, 154, 138, 139, 140, 141, 142, 126, 125, 124, 123, 122, 121, 120, 104, 88, 72, 73, 74, 58, 42, 26, 25, 24, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 31, 30, 29, 28, 27, 43, 59, 75, 76, 60, 44, 45, 46, 47, 63, 79, 95, 94, 78, 62, 61, 77, 93, 92, 91, 90, 89, 105, 106, 107, 108, 109, 110, 111, 127, 143, 159, 158, 157, 173, 174, 175, 191, 190, 189, 205, 221, 237, 238, 222, 206, 207, 223, 239, 255, 254, 253, 252, 251, 250, 234, 235, 236, 220, 204, 188, 187, 186, 185, 184, 183, 199, 200, 201, 202, 203, 219, 218, 217, 216, 215, 231, 232, 233, 249, 248, 247, 246, 230, 214, 213, 229, 245, 244, 243, 242, 241, 240, 224, 208, 192, 193, 209, 225, 226, 210, 194, 195, 211, 227, 228, 212, 196, 197, 198, 182, 181, 180, 179, 178, 177, 176, 160, 161, 162, 146, 130, 114, 98, 82, 81, 97, 113, 129, 145, 144, 128, 112, 96, 80, 64, 48, 32, 16
+```
+
+`verify(inst, inst["answer"])` returns `(True, "ok")`. Dropping the final `16` returns `(False, "wrong length: expected 256 vertex IDs, got 255")`.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| Preset | `n` | Grid | Vertices | Structural-space digits | Status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| `standard` | 8 | 16×16 | 256 | 252 | **ships; oracle held** |
+| `hard` | 11 | 22×22 | 484 | 544 | available; not reached |
+| `extreme` | 15 | 30×30 | 900 | 1,133 | available; not reached |
+
+The structural space is `(m-1)!·2^(m-1)` for `m` forced pairs, modulo rotation and reversal. `escalate` safely increases `n`; inverse planting keeps every size satisfiable.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| Gate | Measured result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 | 9/9 planted instances verified (3 presets × 3 seeds) |
+| G2 | 5/5 corruptions rejected with 5 distinct reasons |
+| G3 | 256-vertex answer recovered from prose and a Markdown fence |
+| G4 | **0/200,000** valid random candidates; candidates are real complementary grid perfect matchings, so only global one-cycle connectivity is left unenforced |
+| G5 | At `n=2`, seeds 2/3/5 had 1/2/2 solutions out of 645,120 (`1.55e-6` to `3.10e-6`) |
+| G6 | Coarse-2×2 signature, deterministic greedy, and 512-restart randomized greedy each solved **0/8** shipping instances |
+| G7 | Doubling `n` built 1,024 vertices and the plant verified |
+| G8 | 160/160 D4-plus-input-order invariance checks, 160/160 carried-answer checks, and 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+
+The machine-readable measurements are in `selftest_report.json`.
+
+## Oracle hardening loop
+
+The required four-vendor-pool harness stopped at round 0 with `verdict: hardened`; its random draw used three distinct vendors:
+
+| Preset | Seed | Model | Solved? | Recorded reason |
+|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| `standard` | 1719824366 | Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview | No | parsed 255 IDs; expected 256 |
+| `standard` | 1819995523 | Claude Sonnet 5 | No output | exhausted 32,000 tokens in reasoning (`finish_reason=length`) |
+| `standard` | 153202309 | GPT-5.6 Terra | No | parsed tour omitted forced edge `16-17` |
+
+The second row is honestly a length-budget failure, not an incorrect parsed witness. Full replies and timings are in `llm_loop_transcript.jsonl`; `.meta.json` records the pool, master seed, and verdict.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import gen_2508_11570 as grand_tour
+
+params = grand_tour.DIFFICULTY[grand_tour.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY]
+inst = grand_tour.make_instance(seed=42, **params)
+question = grand_tour.render(inst)
+candidate = grand_tour.parse_answer(model_output)
+ok, reason = grand_tour.verify(inst, candidate)
+```
+
+From the repository root, emit verified samples with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2508.11570 20 standard
+```
+
+Run the local gates with `python3 results/2508.11570/gen_2508_11570.py`.
+
+## Caveats
+
+- The paper proves worst-case ASP-completeness, not that every planted distribution is hard. These instances come from boundaries of uniformly sampled coarse-grid spanning trees. The explicit coarse-block attack failed, but a specialized algorithm might exploit a subtler signature.
+- G4 uses randomized augmenting-path perfect matchings. It is much stricter than random vertex permutations: all local edge, degree, uniqueness, and forced-edge constraints already hold. The sampler is not uniform over perfect matchings, however, and 0/200,000 is an empirical result for one shipping instance—not a proof that every seed or every prior has probability below `1e-6`.
+- The panel did not try a full SAT/ILP solver, transfer-matrix dynamic programming, exhaustive backtracking at `n=8`, or a learned attack. The oracle evidence is three medium-effort calls, one of which emitted nothing because its reasoning hit the token cap.
+- Full-grid instances become easy with no forced edges, and become explicit when two forced cycle incidences determine every vertex. Reducing `n` is also unsafe: locally valid random guesses succeeded often in exploratory small cases, which is why no preset is below `n=8`.
+- Coordinates are semantic and fixed by the statement. `canonical_key` exactly removes forced-edge order/orientation and all eight square-grid rotations/reflections; it does not solve abstract graph isomorphism under arbitrary coordinate-destroying vertex permutations, which are not symmetries of this coordinate-defined family.
